{{smaller block|Men acting gregariously are always in extremes; as they are one moment capable of higher courage, so they are liable, the next, to ''baser'' depression.
{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 151.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''4.''' Of little value; coarse in quality; worthless, absolutely or comparatively: as, the ''base'' metals (so called in contrast with the noble or precious metals).
{{smaller block|The harvest white plumb is a ''base'' plumb.
{{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist., § 509.}}
Often has the vein of gold displayed itself amid the ''baser'' ores.
{{right|''Marg. Fuller'', Woman in 19th Cent., p. 15.}}}}
Hence—'''5.''' Fraudulently debased in value; spurious; false: as, ''base'' coin.
{{smaller block|They were compelled to accept ''base'' money in exchange for those commodities they were forced to sell.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Hist. Eng., x.}}}}
'''6.''' Low in scale or rank; of humble origin, grade, or station; wanting dignity or estimation; mean; lowly: as, ''base'' menials.
{{smaller block|''Base'' things of the world, and things which are despised, hath God chosen.
{{right|1 Cor. i. 28.}}
<poem>’Tis the plague of great ones;
Prerogatived are they less than the ''base''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, iii. 3.}}}}
'''7.''' Suitable to or characteristic of a low condition; depressed; abject: as, ''base'' servility.
{{smaller block|<poem>I am fire and air; my other elements
I give to ''baser'' life.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., v. 2.}}}}
'''8.''' Of mean spirit; morally low; without dignity of sentiment: said of persons.
{{smaller block|''Base'' is the slave that pays.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 1.}}
The ''base'' and abject multitude.
{{right|''Junius.''}}}}
'''9.''' Showing or proceeding from a mean spirit: said of things.
{{smaller block|<poem>Him that utter’d nothing ''base''.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', To the Queen.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|The one ''base'' thing in the universe—to receive favors and to render none.
{{right|''Emerson'', Compensation.}}}}
'''10.''' Of illegitimate birth; born out of wedlock.
{{smaller block|Why bastard? wherefore ''base?''{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, i. 2.}}
I din’d with S<sup>r</sup> Rob<sup>t</sup> Paston, since Earle of Yarmouth, and saw the Duke of Verneuille, ''base'' brother to the Q. Mother.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, June 23, 1665.}}}}
'''11.''' Deep; grave: applied to sounds: as, the ''base'' tones of a viol. See ''bass''<sup>3</sup>.
{{smaller block|<poem>The silver sounding instruments did meet
With the ''base'' murmure of the waters fall.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. xii. 71.}}}}
'''12.''' In ''old Eng. law'', not held or holding by honorable tenure: as, a ''base'' estate, that is, an estate held by services not honorable nor ''in capite'', or by villeinage. Such a tenure is called ''base'' or low, and the tenant a ''base'' tenant.—'''13.''' Not classical or refined: as, “''base'' Latin,” ''Fuller''.
{{smaller block|<poem>No Muses aide me needes heretoo to call;
''Base'' is the style, and matter meane withall.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Mother Hub. Tale, l. 44.}}}}

