{{smaller|'''Base bullion.''' See ''bullion''.—'''Base court.''' See ''base-court''.—'''Base fee, infeftment, right,''' etc. See the nouns.—'''Base metals.''' See ''metal''. ='''Syn.''' Ignoble, vulgar, plebeian, mean, contemptible, despicable, abject, sordid, groveling, servile, slavish, menial, rascally, villainous.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1†.''' A plaited skirt, reaching from the waist to the knee, worn during the first half of
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Base of rich stuff, the border embroidered; beginning of 16th century.—From tomb of Maximilian I. at Innspruck.}}}}
the sixteenth century. {{smaller|In civil costume it was appended to the doublet, or secured to the girdle; it was also worn over armor.}} '''2†.''' A skirt of plate-armor, corrugated or ribbed vertically, as if in imitation of the preceding. See ''lamboys''.—'''3†.''' The skirt of a woman’s outer garment. The word was used throughout the seventeenth and part of the eighteenth century.—'''4†.''' An apron.
{{smaller block|With gauntlets blue and ''bases'' white.
{{right|''S. Butler'', Hudibras, I. ii. 769.}}
Bakers in their linen ''bases''.
{{right|''Marston.''}}}}
'''5†.''' The housing of a horse: used in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries.
{{smaller block|The ''bases'' and bardes of their horse were grene sattyn.
{{right|''Hall'', Hen. VIII., an. 1.}}}}
